# Current BVH

Horse's existing BVH is **not** the Rasterizer's normal visibility system. It is a triangle-level acceleration structure in `Renderer::Scenes::SceneCache`, currently used by GI ray queries and by the experimental BVH visualizer.

Current build:

```text
scene render items
    ↓
world-space triangles
    ↓
triangle centroids
    ↓
choose largest centroid extent X/Y/Z
    ↓
median split with std::nth_element
    ↓
binary AABB tree
```

- `GpuNode` contains an AABB.
- Internal nodes store two child indices.
- Leaf nodes use `LeafBit` and own a contiguous triangle range.
- The current default effectively allows one triangle per leaf because `leaf_size_` defaults to zero and build uses `max(leaf_size_, 1)`.
- Topology changes rebuild the tree.
- Transform/animation/deformation-only changes update world triangles and refit node AABBs bottom-up without rebuilding topology.
- GI traversal uses CPU AABB slab tests followed by Möller-Trumbore triangle tests.
- Closest-hit traversal currently pushes both children rather than near-child-first sorting.
- GI also uses the same structure for shadow/occlusion checks from probe hits toward lights.
- `Debug -> Experimental -> Show BVH` visualizes AABBs by tree depth.

Do not use this triangle BVH as the Rasterizer's main occlusion system. Raster visibility should use object/instance bounds + Hi-Z on the GPU.

Future GI BVH direction, after the rasterizer foundation is complete:

```text
GI Acceleration
├── TLAS
│   └── scene instances / transforms
│
└── BLAS
    └── reusable mesh-local triangles

Builder
├── binned SAH
├── 4-8 triangles per leaf
└── static/dynamic separation

Traversal
├── near child first
├── closest-hit early pruning
└── specialized any-hit path for occlusion
```

This avoids baking duplicate mesh instances into world-space triangle arrays and makes static mesh acceleration reusable.

