## Phase 9 — Global Illumination Cleanup

**Goal:** Keep GI optional and visually meaningful without exposing algorithm internals.

- Debug exposes Enabled, Quality and Strength only.
- Quality presets own rays/probe budgets/probe dimensions/bounce budgets/photon limits internally.
- Photon mapping remains an implementation choice rather than a player-facing toggle unless a later visual mode genuinely requires it.
- Investigate baked lightmaps + SH/light probes for static scenes before making dynamic GI more expensive.
- Only after profiling, consider TLAS/BLAS + SAH BVH upgrade described above.

- [ ] Add GI quality presets.
- [ ] Remove low-level GAME Debug controls.
- [ ] Profile current CPU GI/BVH cost.
- [ ] Decide dynamic GI vs baked/static-probe split from measured data.

## Phase 10 — Volumetrics Upgrade

**Goal:** Replace raw tuning knobs with quality-driven volumetrics and improve scalability.

Long-term preferred direction: froxel/grid volumetrics rather than brute-force screen work where it offers a real performance/quality improvement.

Player controls:
- Enabled
- Quality
- Density
- Distance

- [ ] Add Volumetrics quality presets.
- [ ] Hide raw resolution/sample/blur controls from normal Debug UI.
- [ ] Profile existing implementation.
- [ ] Evaluate froxel migration only from measured results.

## Phase 11 — Lightweight Render Graph

**Goal:** Keep pass/resource ownership clean as the rasterizer grows.

The graph should describe dependencies and resource lifetime; it must not become a giant abstraction layer around SDL_GPU.

Example:

```text
Depth
 ├── Hi-Z
 ├── Shadows
 └── AO

Visibility
 └── Opaque

Lighting
 ├── Opaque
 └── Volumetrics

Opaque
 ├── Reflections
 └── Transparent
```

- [ ] Introduce only when enough passes exist to remove actual duplication/resource lifetime complexity.
- [ ] Keep API small and code-first.

